  "de5eb605-b32e-4106-8c96-b1e1222599c0": {
   "id": "de5eb605-b32e-4106-8c96-b1e1222599c0",
   "message": {
    "id": "de5eb605-b32e-4106-8c96-b1e1222599c0",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1778628634.741,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Most likely cable tension (barrel adjuster) or a worn chain/cassette. In order:\n\n1. **Indexing (5 min):** shift to the smallest cog, then up one. If it hesitates to climb, turn the **barrel adjuster** (at the derailleur or the shifter) counter-clockwise ¼ turn at a time until it shifts cleanly. If it overshoots, clockwise. Check across all gears.\n2. **Chain wear:** use a chain checker (or measure: 12 links should be 12\" exactly; ≥ 12 1/16\" means replace). A worn chain skips under load, especially on the cogs you use most.\n3. **Cassette wear:** if a new chain skips on certain cogs, those cogs are worn. Replace the cassette.\n4. **Bent derailleur hanger:** if indexing is fine in some gears but off in others, or the derailleur looks tilted from behind, the hanger may be bent (common after a tip-over). Needs a hanger alignment tool or a shop (~$15).\n5. **Cable/housing:** frayed cable or gritty housing makes indexing drift. New cable + housing is cheap.\n\nStart with 1 and 2. Most mid-cassette skipping under load is a stretched chain plus indexing."
     ]
    },
