  "6967e8ca-3752-4235-bb2e-9aae4c31f73f": {
   "id": "6967e8ca-3752-4235-bb2e-9aae4c31f73f",
   "message": {
    "id": "6967e8ca-3752-4235-bb2e-9aae4c31f73f",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1778628785.92,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "That's the classic sign the cassette is worn too. A new chain doesn't mesh with the worn teeth on the cogs you've used most, so it skips there. Replace the cassette (105 11-speed, same range). You'll need a cassette lockring tool and a chain whip, or let a shop do just that part.\n\nLesson for next time: replace chains around 0.5% wear, and the cassette usually lasts 2–3 chains."
     ]
    },
